# Instruction tests that do not pass with the core-only runner on macOS.
# Used by Scripts/macos_asm_tests.py in addition to upstream's lists.
#
# Background: macOS on Apple silicon reserves the first 4GB of every 64-bit process, so
# the tests' fixed low addresses are moved up by 1TB before assembly, and the loader
# places the test's code at 0x100_0001_0000 instead of 0x1_0000. Guest addresses are host
# addresses, so nothing can be mapped where an address cut to 32 bits lands.
#
# Format: the test, then how it must fail. The script reports any other failure of a
# listed test as unexpected.
#   crash fault=<address> signal=SIGSEGV|SIGBUS where=host|translated
#                           the process dies of a host fault at that address, with the pc
#                           in host code or in translated code. The runner prints
#                           "runner: unclaimed SIGSEGV at address <a>, pc <p> in host code
#                           (ESR class <c>, fault status <s>)" for every fault the fexmac
#                           library does not claim, and results.tsv keeps that line.
#                           fault= is required.
#   guest-fault | unsupported-instruction  signal=<n> trap=<n> si_code=<n> at=<rip> [fault=<a>]
#                           the run stops with that fexmac stop reason: a guest exception
#                           (FEXMAC_STOP_GUEST_FAULT; trap 14 is a page fault in translated
#                           code at fault address <a>, si_code 1 SEGV_MAPERR: nothing
#                           mapped there), or code the translator cannot translate
#                           (FEXMAC_STOP_UNSUPPORTED_INSTRUCTION). Runner line "Stopped:".
#                           Before guest fault delivery (package D) the page faults below
#                           were crashes at the same addresses, data aborts in translated
#                           code.
#
# Every entry was checked on an M4 by running it in all four modes; each faulting address
# is the moved address cut to 32 bits, exactly as stated. None is a translator fault.

# A 32-bit address-size override (0x67) cuts the address to 32 bits; the result is below
# 4GB and cannot be mapped. a32_lods and a32_rep_lods: RSI 0x1_0008_0000 cut to 0x80000,
# as the test intends. A6_addrmod and A6_REP_addrmod: RDI 0x61626364_10000200 cut to
# EDI. AE_addrmod: EDI 0x10000100. All page faults in translated code, delivered as stops.
Test_FEX_bugs/a32_lods.asm               guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x1000001003e fault=0x80000
Test_FEX_bugs/a32_rep_lods.asm           guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x1000001005a fault=0x80000
Test_Primary/Primary_A6_REP_addrmod.asm  guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x10000010039 fault=0x10000200
Test_Primary/Primary_A6_addrmod.asm      guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x1000001002e fault=0x10000200
Test_Primary/Primary_AE_addrmod.asm      guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x10000010019 fault=0x10000100

# The same, in the middle of a test of vector moves: `vmovd xmm2, [edx]` with RDX
# 0x100_e000_0000.
Test_VEX/vmovq_vmovd_reg.asm             guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x10000010056 fault=0xe0000000

# Far jumps and calls through an m16:32 pointer: the test stores `lea edi, [rel .success]`,
# the code address cut to 32 bits, as the target. The translator reads a 32-bit offset
# (also for the o64 forms, as AMD does), so control goes to 0x10028, the original code
# address of .success. The fault is in host code (the translator reading the guest
# instructions there; the pc is in the runner, not the code buffer). The o32 forms fault
# first, in translated code, reading the pointer through `[esp]`.
# Known upstream behaviour, not a macOS fault: FEX's CPUID reports an Intel CPU
# (GenuineIntel), but it reads a 32-bit far-pointer offset even with REX.W, which is AMD's
# behaviour (Intel reads m16:64). The intended guest, a console with an AMD CPU, expects
# AMD's.
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05.asm            crash fault=0x10028 signal=SIGSEGV where=host
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o32.asm     guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x10000010023 fault=0xe0000ff0
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o32_imm.asm guest-fault signal=11 trap=14 si_code=1 at=0x10000010023 fault=0xe0000ff0
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o64.asm     crash fault=0x10028 signal=SIGSEGV where=host
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o64_imm.asm crash fault=0x10028 signal=SIGSEGV where=host

# Also on upstream's Known_Failures_jit; listed here to state how it fails on macOS. FEX
# refuses `int 0x80` in a 64-bit process ("[Unsupported] Trying to execute 32-bit
# syscall from a 64-bit process"), so no block can be compiled at the instruction (code
# offset 0x1d). On macOS the run stops there with FEXMAC_STOP_UNSUPPORTED_INSTRUCTION
# (filled like #UD: SIGILL, trap 6, but si_code 0); upstream, and this port before,
# jumped to host address 0.
Test_FEX_bugs/32bit_syscall.asm          unsupported-instruction signal=4 trap=6 si_code=0 at=0x1000001001d

# --- Port tests (unittests/macOS/ASM) whose correct result is a stop, not a halt.

# A real invalid opcode stops as a guest #UD (ILL_ILLOPN, si_code 2) ...
Test_macOS/ud2.asm                       guest-fault signal=4 trap=6 si_code=2 at=0x1000001000f
# ... and a valid instruction FEX does not implement stops as unsupported (si_code 0).
Test_macOS/mov_fs.asm                    unsupported-instruction signal=4 trap=6 si_code=0 at=0x1000001000f
# The same through the single-step (trap flag) compile path.
Test_macOS/tf_int80.asm                  unsupported-instruction signal=4 trap=6 si_code=0 at=0x10000010019
